\viewkind4\uc1\pard\f0\fs20 CHAPTER ONE\par
\par
The rain had been falling since before dawn, a thin grey curtain that\par
hung over the harbour and blurred the masts of the fishing boats into\par
a single smudge of charcoal. Margaret stood at the window of the old\par
customs house with her hands pressed flat against the cold glass,\par
watching for a sail that she already knew would not come.\par
\par
She had been told, of course. Everyone in the village had made a point\par
of telling her, in the careful, well-meaning voices people reserve for\par
the bereaved and the slightly mad. The Albatross was three weeks\par
overdue; the storm off the Hebrides had been the worst in forty years;\par
there was no sense in hoping.\par
\par
"You'll catch your death standing there," said Mrs. Pennywhistle from\par
the doorway.\par
\par
"Then I shall have caught something, at least."\par
\par
The older woman clicked her tongue and came to stand beside her. For a\par
long moment neither of them spoke. Somewhere below, a door banged in\par
the wind, and a dog began to bark with the monotonous persistence of\par
an animal that has forgotten why it started.\par
\par
"He wrote to me," Margaret said at last. "Before they sailed. Did I\par
ever tell you that? A ridiculous letter, all about the weather and the\par
cook's terrible porridge and a gull that had taken a liking to his\par
hat."\par
\par
"Sounds like him."\par
\par
"It does." She smiled, though it did not reach her eyes. "It sounds\par
exactly like him."\par
\par
By noon the rain had thinned to a drizzle and the light had taken on\par
that peculiar pewter quality that belongs only to coastal towns in\par
late October. Margaret walked the length of the quay twice, past the\par
lobster pots stacked like the vertebrae of some enormous beast, past\par
the chandler's shop with its faded sign, past the bench where old Tom\par
Hallett sat every afternoon regardless of the weather, mending nets\par
that no one would ever use again.\par
\par
"Afternoon, Miss Carrow."\par
\par
"Afternoon, Tom."\par
\par
"Bit of a blow last night."\par
\par
"Yes."\par
\par
He looked up at her then, his eyes pale and watery beneath the brim of\par
his cap. "They'll be sheltering, like as not. Up past Stornoway--\par
there's coves up there a man could hide a fleet in. My own father was\par
lost nine days once, and came home with a beard down to here and a\par
barrel of salt herring he'd traded off a Norwegian."\par
\par
It was a kindness, and she recognised it as one. She thanked him and\par
walked on, and did not let herself cry until she had turned the corner\par
by the church and there was no one to see.\par
\par
That night she dreamed of the sea. Not the grey, rain-pocked sea of\par
the harbour, but a vast and luminous expanse under a sky crowded with\par
stars, and on it a single ship with every sail set, moving silently\par
northward toward a horizon that kept retreating, and retreating, and\par
never came any nearer at all.\par
\par
In the morning there was a knock at the door.\par
\par
